<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<template xmlns="http://schemas.openehr.org/v1">
    <id>
        <value>lifestyle_medicine_hrv.v0</value>
    </id>
    <name>
        <value>Lifestyle Medicine HRV Assessment</value>
    </name>
    <description>
        <original_author>
            <item>
                <key>name</key>
                <value>Ricardo Lourenco dos Santos</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>organisation</key>
                <value>Faculty of Medicine, University of Porto (FMUP)</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>email</key>
                <value>ricardolourencosantos@gmail.com</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>date</key>
                <value>2026-01-29</value>
            </item>
        </original_author>
        <lifecycle_state>in_development</lifecycle_state>
        <details>
            <purpose>To record heart rate variability (HRV) measurements from consumer wearable devices for autonomic nervous system assessment and stress/recovery monitoring in lifestyle medicine practice.</purpose>
            <keywords>HRV, heart rate variability, SDNN, RMSSD, autonomic nervous system, stress, recovery, wearable, smartwatch</keywords>
            <use>Use for recording HRV measurements including:
- Time-domain metrics: SDNN, RMSSD, pNN50, mean NN interval
- Frequency-domain metrics: LF power, HF power, LF/HF ratio
- Poincare plot: SD1, SD2
- Recording context: posture, activity state, time of day
- Device provenance: source device, firmware, measurement quality

Primary use cases:
1. Morning readiness assessment (recovery from previous day)
2. Pre/post exercise HRV comparison
3. Sleep-time HRV analysis
4. Longitudinal stress monitoring

Compatible devices: Apple Watch Series 4+, Polar H10, Garmin, Oura Ring, Whoop</use>
            <misuse>Not for:
- Diagnosing cardiac arrhythmias (requires medical-grade ECG)
- Clinical autonomic function testing (requires controlled conditions)
- Emergency cardiac assessment</misuse>
            <copyright>CC BY-SA 4.0</copyright>
        </details>
        <other_details>
            <item>
                <key>licence</key>
                <value>Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0)</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>custodian_organisation</key>
                <value>FMUP - Faculty of Medicine, University of Porto</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>references</key>
                <value>1. Shaffer F, Ginsberg JP. An Overview of Heart Rate Variability Metrics and Norms. Front Public Health. 2017;5:258.
2. Task Force of ESC and NASPE. Heart rate variability: standards of measurement. Circulation. 1996;93(5):1043-65.
3. Buchheit M. Monitoring training status with HR measures. Sports Med. 2014;44(Suppl 2):S185-97.
4. Plews DJ, et al. Training adaptation and heart rate variability in elite endurance athletes. Int J Sports Physiol Perform. 2013;8(6):688-91.</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>FHIR_mapping</key>
                <value>Maps to HRVObservation profile in iOS Lifestyle Medicine FHIR IG. LOINC codes: SDNN (80404-7), Heart rate (8867-4)</value>
            </item>
        </other_details>
    </description>
